  "acceptable_answers": [
    "Step down to oral trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole or ciprofloxacin, both susceptible, which also avoids the AmpC question.",
    "Change ceftriaxone to cefepime because of the AmpC risk in an upper tract infection, then step down orally.",
    "Continue ceftriaxone, given clinical response and no bacteremia, and step down orally when ready."
  ],
  "reference_answer": "Flag both the oral step-down and the AmpC concern. Oral TMP-SMX or a fluoroquinolone is the simplest answer that resolves both; changing to cefepime or continuing ceftriaxone in a responding patient is defensible.",
  "rationale": "An ambiguous case: whether pyelonephritis counts as an invasive infection for the AmpC caution, and whether a clinical response on ceftriaxone changes it, is where reasonable pharmacists differ. Fully susceptible oral agents make step-down attractive.",
